<main>
<div class="eyebrow">Real-Time Sound Visualizer &middot; a listening study</div>
<h1>One note to two: what happens to the first note</h1>
<p>The owner's report, 16 November, on Harmonic tone: <em>a bass note in the left, then a higher note in the right, then back
to just the bass &mdash; the first note dips in and out of the ears; its width and level change instantly when another note is
added.</em> Every take here is the instrument itself, the core rendered offline through
<code>core/tests/tools/render_cpp.cpp</code>, and every option is a scratch copy of the core's headers: nothing in the instrument
has changed. The full write-up is <code>web/docs/one-to-two-notes.md</code>.</p>
<p class="small">The phrase in every take: C2 alone from 0.2 s, E4 added at 1.8 s and let go at 3.4 s, C2 alone again until 5.2 s,
then its release. Harmonic tone as the bank has it (it plays in Dyad, the mode's default, since it names none), the picture at
96 with its Swell. <strong>Stereo, and best on headphones</strong>: the issue is between the ears. 16-bit, 44.1 kHz, six seconds
each. <span id="status" class="status"></span></p>

<section class="sheet">
<div class="eyebrow">What happens today</div>
<h2>The bass's place is decided by what else is held</h2>
<p>A note alone is drawn as a ring: its right channel is the same note a quarter of a cycle ahead. To the ear that is the widest
a single note can be &mdash; both ears equally loud and not moving together (coherence nought). A second note changes that, in a
different way in each mode:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Dyad</strong> (Harmonic tone): the left channel is the lower note and the right the upper, so the bass's right
ear stops being the bass at all &mdash; it is gone from the right <em>at the sample the key goes down</em>. When the E is let go
the right becomes the bass again, but its phase has run on at five times the speed all the while, so the bass comes back at
whatever angle the release happened to catch: anywhere from the same in both ears (narrow, centred) to opposite (wide,
hollow) &mdash; never, except by chance, the ring it started as. That is the &ldquo;doesn't come back the same&rdquo;.</li>
<li><strong>Poly</strong>: the lowest and highest notes lean to their sides, full on their own and half on the other, and only a
note alone or an inner voice gets the quarter turn. So the bass goes from both ears at full, a quarter apart, to its right ear
6 dB down <em>and moving in step with its left</em> &mdash; narrower and to the left &mdash; in 4 ms, and back when the E goes.
It does come back as it started.</li>
<li><strong>A split</strong>: each hand is its own figure, so the bass stays a ring whatever the right hand does.</li>
</ul>
