<section class="sheet">
<div class="eyebrow">What does not cause it</div>
<h2>Ruled out, with numbers</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Level normalisation by voice count:</strong> there is none; a voice is summed at its own level. With the picture off
the bass's left ear reads &minus;11.22 dB alone, with the E and after it, in Dyad, to the hundredth.</li>
<li><strong>The envelope:</strong> nothing is restarted; the bass's left ear does not move by a hundredth of a dB at either event
in Dyad. In Poly the voice is kept by its note and never reborn or re-phased.</li>
<li><strong>The limiter:</strong> at most 0.06 dB of reduction while the two are held (0.3 dB in the split).</li>
<li><strong>The picture swinging the image:</strong> its Twist turns the figure, which would swing a narrow image between the ears;
measured, the balance between the ears moves by under 1.5 dB however the bass came back.</li>
<li><strong>The picture changing the timbre at once:</strong> it does change it &mdash; the E doubles what is lit (cover 0.17 to
0.34) and the loop's Fold and Echo follow &mdash; but no faster than the sound's own breathing: Fold moves 0.02 to 0.03 in the
100 ms after the E, where it moves 0.03 in 100 ms of its own; Echo 0.04 to 0.08 against its own 0.19 to 0.24. That is Breathing doing
what it says, and it is the same in every version below (the last table).</li>
<li><strong>Scan's table:</strong> Harmonic tone has Scan off. On the bank's Poly presets it is on, and there it is a real,
slower cause: on Tine piano the held bass gets up to 6 dB louder over the 0.4 s after the E, because Scan reads the two-note
figure into every voice (0.0 dB with Scan off). It glides with the screen's persistence and is the picture shaping the sound,
so no option touches it.</li>
</ul>
</section>

<section class="sheet">
<div class="eyebrow">The options</div>
<h2>A and B for Dyad, C and D for Poly</h2>
<p><strong>A.</strong> When the dyad's interval changes, put the right channel back in step with the left (at the new ratio, from the
left's phase) and crossfade the old right into the new over 30 ms, so it neither clicks nor jumps. The bass leaves the right as
today, but comes back as the ring it started as, every time; and a dyad reached two ways now draws one figure.
<strong>B.</strong> A, and lean as Poly leans: the bass's own quarter turn stays in the right at half while the E is held, and the
E's own copy goes into the left at half. The bass stays in both ears; Dyad's figure is sheared.</p>
<div class="takes">%OPTIONS_DYAD%</div>
<p><strong>C.</strong> Keep the quarter turn on the outer notes too, not only on a note alone or an inner voice: the bass still leans
6 dB, but keeps its width (coherence nought throughout), and the pair draws an open figure where today it draws a diagonal smear.
<strong>D.</strong> No lean at all: every note on both sides with its own quarter turn, so a note's image never depends on what
else is held. The two notes no longer draw against each other: the figure is their two rings summed.</p>
<div class="takes">%OPTIONS_POLY%</div>
<h3>The bass's own image in each (picture off)</h3>
%OPTIONS_TABLE%
<h3>The picture's share, the same in every version (picture on, as the takes are)</h3>
<p class="small">How far the bass wanders from the same bass held alone through the same six seconds, from the E's entry to
the bass's release: its harmonics' centre (in harmonic numbers; it breathes between about 1.2 and 7 by itself) and its level,
both on average.</p>
